        <body>
            <div xml:id="d59">

                <pb facs="0130" n="82" sameAs="frus:frus1877/pg82"/>

                <head>No. 59.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Seward to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1876-11-17"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 17, 1876</date>.
                        (Received Jan. 3, 1877.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 163.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: It may be interesting for you to read the
                    inclosed translation of an imperial decree confirming a sentence of death upon
                    two officials who have been guilty of falsifying accounts to the extent of
                    49,000 taels, or about $65,000, and directing the board of punishments to
                    determine what penalty shall be inflicted upon certain high officials “for their
                    failure to discover that these frauds were being perpetrated.”</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEORGE F. SEWARD.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d59-a1.xml" xpointer="d59-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
